ager called the paper a \"blueprint for the digital era\". 2001-07-01.†www.technologyreview.com】\n- Gleick, James. The Information: A History, a Theory, a Flood. Vintage Books.\n- Watson, Peter. Convergence: The Idea at the Heart of Science. Simon & Schuster. 2018.\n- Nicolelis, Miguel A. L. The True Creator of Everything: How the Human Brain Shaped the Universe as We Know it. Yale University Press. 2020.\n\n## Publication\n\nThe article was the founding work of the field of information theory. It was later published in 1949 as a book titled The Mathematical Theory of Communication (), which was published as a paperback in 1963 (). The book contains an additional article by Warren Weaver, providing an overview of the theory for a more general audience. \n\n- The Mathematical Theory of Communication.\n\n## Contents\n\nThis work is known for introducing the concepts of channel capacity as well as the noisy channel coding theorem.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMathematical Theory Of Communication (https://sclc2019.iaslc.org/About/virtual-library/Documents/mathematical-theory-of-communication.pdf)\nciteturn423search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; The Mathematical Theory of Communication Claude E Shannon,Warren Weaver,1998-09-01 Scientiﬁc knowledge grows at a ... The University of Illinois Press is pleased and honored to issue this commemorative reprinting of a classic. ... book is ideal for use in the classroom, for self-study, and as a reference for researchers and engineers in industry and academia.\nThe Mathematical Theory of Communication Claude E Shannon,Warren Weaver,1998-09-01 Scientiﬁc knowledge grows at a\nphenomenal pace--but few books have had as lasting an impact or played as important a role in our modern world as The Mathematical\nTheory of Communication, published originally as a paper on communication theory more than ﬁfty years ago. Republished in book form\nshortly thereafter, it has s